# The member login class: the session shell runs confined git on port 2222.
#
# On-box path: /etc/login.conf.d/member. login.conf.d/<class> takes precedence
# over the same class in /etc/login.conf (login.conf(5), FILES), which is the
# convention this box already runs: dovecot and rspamd each ship one there, and
# so does the applicant-facing sshd's onboard class. The drop-in is the whole of
# what this platform puts in the member class, so the base file is never edited
# and a base update cannot conflict with this stanza.
#
# The account's password entry keeps /sbin/nologin. sshd takes the session shell
# from the class instead: auth.c sets lc = login_getclass(pw->pw_class), and
# session.c's do_child() takes shell = login_getcapstr(lc, "shell", pw_shell,
# pw_shell) and execs the session's command through it. Port 2222 also carries
# ForceCommand, so a push arrives as
# `member-git-shell.sh -c '/usr/local/libexec/member-git-shell.sh --force'` with
# the client's command in SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND, while the account has no shell of
# its own in /etc/master.passwd.
#
# The port 22 upload session is unaffected by this class. Its block carries
# ForceCommand internal-sftp, and do_exec() sets is_subsystem =
# SUBSYSTEM_INT_SFTP for that command, so do_child() runs sftp_server_main() in
# process, before the shell is read. One account therefore has both sessions, on
# one key, decided by the port: 22 is the sftp upload and 2222 is confined git.
#
# Why not git-shell(1). The login class used to name /usr/local/bin/git-shell,
# and git-shell re-execs git-receive-pack or git-upload-pack with whatever path
# the client sent. Port 22 is chrooted to /home/www/<user>.kyriakon.net; 2222 is
# not, and the same directory the member owns outside the chroot is where sftp
# uploads land inside it. So a member could upload
# /home/www/<user>.kyriakon.net/www/repo.git with an executable
# hooks/pre-receive (or a config naming core.fsmonitor), push to it on 2222, and
# have git run that command as the member. git-shell also runs executables from
# ~/git-shell-commands as custom commands, and the member owns ~.
#
# /usr/local/libexec/member-git-shell.sh is the replacement. It reads `-c`, takes
# only git-receive-pack, git-upload-pack and git-upload-archive, and refuses any
# repository that is not /home/<user>/repos/<name>.git (resolving the path, so
# `..` and a symlink in ~/repos are refused too). It then execs git with
# `-c core.hooksPath=/dev/null -c core.fsmonitor=false`, which outranks the
# repository's own config, so a hook or a monitor command in the repository
# never runs. A member with no ~/repos/<name>.git can push nowhere else. The
# wrapper is root:wheel 0755, installed by scripts/deploy-mail.sh and again by
# scripts/provision-member.sh; nothing the member can write is on its path.
#
# cap_mkdb(1) has to run after this file is installed or edited whenever
# /etc/login.conf.db exists, because that database is what login_getclass reads
# when it is there (login.conf(5): cap_mkdb "must be run after each edit of
# /etc/login.conf or the /etc/login.conf.d/${class} file to keep the database
# version in sync with the plain file").
#
# scripts/provision-member.sh installs this file and the wrapper from the
# checkout, runs cap_mkdb when the database exists, and then assigns the class:
#
#   doas usermod -L member <username>
#
# which is the step that makes the class reach the account. Without it the
# account keeps the default class and port 2222 runs /sbin/nologin, which
# ignores -c and exits. By hand, the same steps are:
#
#   doas install -m 0755 -o root -g wheel scripts/member-git-shell.sh /usr/local/libexec/member-git-shell.sh
#   doas install -m 0644 openbsd/etc/login.conf.d/member /etc/login.conf.d/member
#   doas cap_mkdb /etc/login.conf        # only when /etc/login.conf.db exists
#   doas usermod -L member <username>
#
# The stanza is one line rather than the backslash-continued form the onboard
# class uses: the two are the same to login.conf(5), and this one is short enough
# to read in one. The spec writes the string the ticket prints, with a printf
# escape in it, and reads the interpolation capability as c=default and the shell
# as \shell; neither is a capability name login.conf(5) has. The capability that
# interpolates another class is tc and the session shell is shell, so the drop-in
# carries those two, with the interpolation last the way the onboard class and
# the shipped /etc/login.conf both write it.

member:shell=/usr/local/libexec/member-git-shell.sh:tc=default:
